<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Nabataean Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">168 BCE – 106 CE (approx.) · capital Petra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Nabataean Kingdom, an Arab trading state that controlled the incense routes and carved its capital Petra out of rose-red rock.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Nabataeans were Arab nomads who controlled desert routes from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. By the 4th century BCE they were rich traders, and by 168 BCE they had a king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-i">Aretas I</a>, who appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/books-of-maccabees">Books of Maccabees</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>, in the mountains of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/edom">Edom</a>, became their capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nabataean kings ruled through control of the frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a> trade, protected caravans and built cisterns and channels to store desert water. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra's</a> facades, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khazneh">Al-Khazneh</a>, mix Greek, Egyptian and local styles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-iii">Aretas III</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and the Nabataean script developed into the Arabic alphabet.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom became a Roman client and declined as sea trade grew. When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabbel-ii">Rabbel II</a> died, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> annexed it in 106 CE as the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia-petraea">Arabia Petraea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nabataean-kingdom.jpg" alt="Nabataean Kingdom" width="320" height="211"><figcaption>Image: SVG map by Ali Zifan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nabatean_Kingdom_(flat_map).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>168 BCE – 106 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Petra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aretas III, Aretas IV, Rabbel II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Nabataean religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nabataean Aramaic, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a>, <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nabataea, Nabataeans</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-31&amp;focus=nabataean-kingdom" aria-label="Nabataean Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5599 1071 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nabataean Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5919 1327L5304 942M5919 1327L6000 1457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M6000 1457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5919 1327h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5919 1327h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Petra. <a href="/atlas?year=-31&amp;focus=nabataean-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
